-- ID 45: Bonds and Debt — Discount vs premium issuance effect on interest expense over bond life
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Manufacturing issues two separate $1,000,000 face value, 10-year bond series on the same date. Series A is issued at 96 (discount) and Series B is issued at 104 (premium). Both carry a stated rate of 6% and pay interest annually. Over the 10-year life of each bond, how does the issuance price affect the total interest expense recognized, and what does the difference reveal about the relationship between carrying amount, stated rate, and effective cost of borrowing?',
  choices = '["Total interest expense is the same for both series ($600,000) because both have the same stated rate and face value — the discount and premium affect only the balance sheet, not the income statement", "Series A''s total interest expense ($640,000) exceeds Series B''s ($560,000) because the discount represents additional borrowing cost amortized over the bond term — the effective interest rate for Series A exceeds 6% while Series B''s effective rate is below 6%, demonstrating that the market demanded higher compensation from Series A''s lower issuance price", "Series B''s total interest expense is higher because the premium increases the carrying amount, which increases the base for computing interest expense under the effective interest method", "Both series produce the same total interest expense because the effective interest method produces a constant dollar amount of expense each period regardless of whether the bond was issued at a discount or premium"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Total cash interest for both = $1,000,000 x 6% x 10 = $600,000. Series A: total expense = $600,000 + $40,000 discount amortization = $640,000 (effective rate > 6%). Cash received was only $960,000, so the extra $40,000 in expense represents the cost of borrowing at below-market terms. Series B: total expense = $600,000 - $40,000 premium amortization = $560,000 (effective rate < 6%). Cash received was $1,040,000, so the $40,000 premium reduces total cost. The discount/premium is the market''s pricing mechanism — a discount signals that the stated rate was below market at issuance, requiring additional compensation through the amortized discount. (A) is wrong because discount/premium amortization directly affects interest expense per ASC 835. (C) is wrong because premium amortization reduces interest expense, not increases it. (D) is wrong because the effective interest method produces a constant rate, not a constant dollar amount.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 45;

-- ID 46: Bonds and Debt — Effective interest method divergence from cash payment and its financial statement effect
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greystone Corp. issues a $500,000, 5-year, 6% bond when the market rate is 8%. The bond pays interest semiannually. The issue price is $459,342. In the first semiannual period, the cash interest payment is $15,000 and the interest expense under the effective interest method is $18,374. How does the $3,374 difference between expense and cash payment affect Greystone''s balance sheet and income statement, and what pattern will this difference follow over the remaining nine periods?',
  choices = '["The $3,374 difference is recorded as additional interest expense on the income statement but has no balance sheet effect because it is a non-cash adjustment", "The $3,374 increases the carrying amount of the bond liability (from $459,342 to $462,716) because the discount amortization represents unpaid borrowing cost being added to the principal — over the remaining periods, this difference will increase each period because the growing carrying amount produces progressively larger interest expense while the cash payment remains fixed at $15,000", "The $3,374 reduces the carrying amount of the bond because the discount is being consumed, and the difference will remain constant across all periods under the effective interest method", "The $3,374 is recorded as a deferred financing cost asset that is amortized separately from interest expense over the bond term"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under the effective interest method (ASC 835-30), interest expense = carrying amount x market rate per period = $459,342 x 4% = $18,374. Cash paid = face x stated rate per period = $500,000 x 3% = $15,000. The $3,374 difference is the discount amortization, which increases the bond''s carrying amount from $459,342 to $462,716. Each subsequent period, the higher carrying amount produces a larger interest expense (e.g., period 2: $462,716 x 4% = $18,509), while the cash payment stays fixed at $15,000. This accelerating pattern continues until the carrying amount reaches $500,000 at maturity and the final period''s discount amortization is the largest. (A) is wrong because the discount amortization increases the bond liability on the balance sheet. (C) reverses the direction — discount amortization increases carrying amount toward face value. (D) is wrong because the discount is a contra-liability, not a deferred cost asset.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 46;

-- ID 168: Bonds and Debt — Straight-line vs effective interest method effect on income statement comparability
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Corp. issues $2,000,000 of 10-year bonds at 106. Its CFO considers using the straight-line method for premium amortization because it is simpler. The company''s auditor notes that ASC 835-30 prefers the effective interest method. Under straight-line, annual premium amortization is $12,000 and interest expense is constant at $108,000 per year (assuming 6% stated rate). Under the effective interest method, Year 1 interest expense would be $103,600 and Year 10 expense would be $113,200. What is the analytical effect of choosing straight-line over effective interest, and when is the straight-line method acceptable?',
  choices = '["The straight-line method always produces materially different results and is never acceptable under U.S. GAAP — the effective interest method is the only permitted approach", "The straight-line method produces constant annual expense ($108,000), which understates interest expense in the early years and overstates it in later years relative to the effective interest method — this distortion flattens the expense pattern and misrepresents the declining effective cost of borrowing as the premium amortizes, but the method is acceptable when the results are not materially different from the effective interest method", "The straight-line method is preferred over the effective interest method because it produces more predictable earnings and better matches interest expense with revenue over the bond term", "Both methods produce identical total interest expense and identical annual expense — the choice is purely a disclosure preference with no financial statement impact"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Total interest expense is identical under both methods ($1,080,000 over 10 years), but the timing differs. Straight-line records $108,000 each year. The effective interest method starts at $103,600 (Year 1) and increases to $113,200 (Year 10) because premium amortization reduces the carrying amount, which reduces the interest expense component calculated as carrying amount times market rate. In early years, straight-line overstates expense relative to effective interest; in later years, it understates expense. The effective interest method better reflects the economic cost of borrowing at each point. ASC 835-30-35 permits straight-line only when results are not materially different from the effective interest method. (A) is wrong because straight-line is acceptable when immaterial. (C) is wrong because the effective interest method is the GAAP-preferred approach. (D) is wrong because while total expense is the same, annual expense amounts differ.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 168;

