-- NOT-FOR-PROFIT ACCOUNTING (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 53: Not-for-Profit — Two-category net asset model effect on financial statement transparency
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The Thornfield Community Foundation, a not-for-profit organization, reports the following net asset balances after adopting ASU 2016-14: net assets without donor restrictions of $2.8 million and net assets with donor restrictions of $4.5 million. The $4.5 million includes $1.2 million of time-restricted operating grants expiring next year, $800,000 of purpose-restricted capital campaign funds, and $2.5 million in perpetual endowments. A new board member asks how the simplified two-category system provides sufficient transparency compared to the old three-category model. What analytical information is gained and lost under the two-category system?',
  choices = '["The two-category system provides identical information because the old three categories map directly to the two new categories with no loss of detail", "The two-category system improves transparency by eliminating the confusing distinction between temporarily and permanently restricted net assets, but it consolidates $1.2 million of near-term spendable assets with $2.5 million of perpetual endowments into one category — financial statement users must rely on note disclosures to distinguish time restrictions expiring next year from perpetual endowment restrictions, making the notes essential for assessing the Foundation''s available liquidity", "The two-category system reduces transparency because it provides less information in all respects — the FASB should not have eliminated the three-category model", "The two-category system eliminates the need for note disclosures about donor restrictions because all restricted amounts are aggregated into a single line item"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ASU 2016-14 (ASC 958-210) replaced the three-category model (unrestricted, temporarily restricted, permanently restricted) with two categories: with and without donor restrictions. This simplification eliminates a source of preparer confusion (distinguishing temporary from permanent restrictions) but aggregates economically different items — time restrictions expiring next year ($1.2M, essentially available soon) and perpetual endowments ($2.5M, never spendable as principal) — into a single line. The standard compensates by requiring enhanced note disclosures about the nature, amount, and timing of restrictions, and about the endowment''s composition and spending policies. Without reading the notes, an analyst cannot assess how much of the $4.5M will become available for operations. (A) is wrong because the aggregation does lose face-of-statement detail. (C) is overly broad — the two-category system provides simpler face presentation with enhanced notes. (D) is wrong because ASU 2016-14 increased note disclosure requirements, not eliminated them.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 53;

-- ID 182: Not-for-Profit — Conditional vs unconditional promises and their effect on revenue timing
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Youth Services receives two pledges on October 1, 2025. Pledge A is a $500,000 unconditional promise to give, payable in two equal installments over 2026-2027. Pledge B is a $500,000 conditional promise contingent on the organization raising $1 million from other donors by June 30, 2026 (a matching grant). As of December 31, 2025, the organization has raised $400,000 toward the matching requirement. How does the conditional vs unconditional distinction affect the timing of revenue recognition for each pledge, and what is the balance sheet impact at year-end 2025?',
  choices = '["Both pledges are recognized as revenue of $500,000 each in 2025 because the matching condition is likely to be met based on progress toward the $1 million goal", "Pledge A is recognized at its present value as contribution revenue with donor restrictions (time restriction) in 2025, creating a pledges receivable asset — Pledge B is not recognized as revenue because the condition (raising $1 million) has not been substantially met, and the $400,000 progress is disclosed but does not trigger recognition — this creates a $500,000 difference in reported revenue from two identical dollar amounts", "Both pledges are deferred until cash is received because not-for-profit organizations use the cash basis of accounting for contributions", "Pledge A is recognized at $500,000 (undiscounted) and Pledge B is recognized at $400,000 to reflect the 80% progress toward the matching condition"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 958-605, unconditional promises to give are recognized as revenue when the promise is made, measured at the present value of expected future cash flows. Pledge A''s two-year payment schedule creates a time restriction, classifying the revenue as net assets with donor restrictions. A pledges receivable is recorded at present value. For Pledge B, ASC 958-605-25-11 requires that conditional promises — those with a barrier (matching requirement) and a right of return/release — are not recognized until the condition is substantially met. Raising $400,000 of the required $1 million (40%) is not substantial performance, so Pledge B remains off the books. The distinction is critical: identical dollar amounts produce completely different accounting outcomes based solely on the condition. (A) is wrong because the condition has not been substantially met — probability of meeting it is irrelevant under the standard. (C) is wrong because NFPs use accrual accounting under ASC 958. (D) is wrong because partial recognition of conditional promises is not permitted.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 182;

-- ID 184: Not-for-Profit — Function vs nature expense reporting and its effect on donor and analyst evaluation
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'The Clearwater Health Alliance, a not-for-profit, reports total expenses of $6.2 million. By function: program services $4.3 million, management and general $1.1 million, and fundraising $800,000. By nature within program services: salaries $2.4 million, professional fees $900,000, supplies $600,000, and occupancy $400,000. A major donor reviews the financial statements and expresses concern that the 29.7% ratio of supporting activities to total expenses ($1.9M / $6.2M) is too high. How does the dual presentation of expenses by function and nature under ASU 2016-14 help or hinder the donor''s analysis?',
  choices = '["The dual presentation is redundant because the functional classification alone provides sufficient information to evaluate organizational efficiency", "The dual presentation enables the donor to evaluate both how resources were deployed (function) and what costs were incurred (nature), but the 29.7% supporting-activities ratio can be misleading without context — a high fundraising ratio may reflect startup investment that will generate future contributions, and the nature breakdown reveals that $2.4 million (56%) of program costs are direct salaries, indicating the Alliance is service-delivery-focused rather than grant-pass-through — evaluating either dimension in isolation distorts the efficiency assessment", "The dual presentation harms analysis because it gives donors too much information, making financial statements harder to interpret for non-accountants", "The dual presentation reveals that Clearwater is misallocating resources because management and general expenses ($1.1M) exceed fundraising expenses ($800,000), which is inefficient for a health-focused NFP"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ASU 2016-14 (ASC 958-720-45) requires NFPs to present expenses by both function and nature to give stakeholders a complete picture. The functional breakdown shows resource allocation across programs and support — the 29.7% supporting-activities ratio provides a high-level efficiency metric. But ratios without context mislead: a new organization investing heavily in fundraising infrastructure may have high current ratios that will decline as the donor base matures. The nature breakdown within program services reveals the composition of costs — $2.4 million in salaries indicates direct service delivery rather than subgranting, which donors concerned about program impact find meaningful. Neither dimension alone tells the full story. (A) is wrong because functional classification alone does not reveal cost composition. (C) is wrong because transparency is the purpose of financial reporting standards. (D) is wrong because the relationship between M&G and fundraising varies legitimately by organization type and stage.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 184;

