-- ID 42: Intangible Assets — Indefinite-life vs finite-life impairment testing framework comparison
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Pemberton Media acquired two intangible assets in a business combination: a broadcast license valued at $3.5 million with an indefinite useful life and a customer list valued at $2.8 million with a 7-year useful life. Three years after the acquisition, the broadcast license has a fair value of $2.9 million and the customer list has a carrying amount of $1.6 million with undiscounted expected future cash flows of $1.7 million and a fair value of $1.3 million. How does the impairment testing framework differ between these two assets, and what impairment loss, if any, is recognized for each?',
  choices = '["Both assets use the same impairment test — compare carrying amount to fair value — resulting in a $600,000 loss on the broadcast license and a $300,000 loss on the customer list", "The broadcast license uses a one-step test (carrying amount vs fair value under ASC 350) resulting in a $600,000 impairment loss — the customer list uses a two-step test under ASC 360 where Step 1 compares carrying amount to undiscounted cash flows, and since $1.7 million exceeds $1.6 million, the asset is recoverable and no impairment is recognized despite the fair value being below carrying amount", "Neither asset is impaired because both fair values exceed zero and the assets continue to generate cash flows", "The broadcast license is not impaired because indefinite-life assets are never written down, while the customer list has a $300,000 impairment loss based on the fair value comparison"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 350-30, indefinite-lived intangible assets (other than goodwill) are impaired when carrying amount exceeds fair value — a single comparison. The broadcast license: $3.5M carrying > $2.9M fair value = $600,000 impairment. Under ASC 360-10 for finite-lived assets, impairment testing requires a two-step approach: Step 1 (recoverability) compares carrying amount to undiscounted future cash flows. The customer list: $1.6M carrying < $1.7M undiscounted cash flows, so the asset passes the recoverability test. Step 2 (measurement against fair value) is performed only if Step 1 fails. Even though fair value ($1.3M) is below carrying amount, no impairment is recognized because the asset passed the recoverability screen. This framework difference is significant — the undiscounted cash flow screen in ASC 360 prevents impairment recognition in situations where the asset''s operational value exceeds its market value. (A) is wrong because finite-lived assets use the two-step test, not a direct fair value comparison. (D) reverses the rules — indefinite-lived assets must be tested for impairment annually.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 42;

-- ID 409: Intangible Assets — IFRS vs U.S. GAAP development cost capitalization: comparability effect
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Thornfield Pharmaceuticals operates in two jurisdictions: its U.S. subsidiary follows U.S. GAAP (ASC 730) and its European subsidiary follows IFRS (IAS 38). Both subsidiaries spent $3.6 million on drug development in Year 2 after establishing technical feasibility and meeting all IAS 38 capitalization criteria. The U.S. subsidiary expenses the entire $3.6 million, while the European subsidiary capitalizes $3.6 million and begins amortization in Year 3. How does this treatment difference affect the comparability of reported assets, net income, and R&D intensity ratios between the two subsidiaries, and what should an analyst do to make them comparable?',
  choices = '["The difference has no analytical significance because both subsidiaries spent the same cash amount on development — the accounting treatment is cosmetic", "The U.S. subsidiary reports $3.6 million lower total assets and $3.6 million lower Year 2 net income than the European subsidiary, inflating the U.S. subsidiary''s R&D-to-revenue ratio while depressing its ROA — in Year 3 and beyond, the European subsidiary''s amortization charges reduce its income while the U.S. subsidiary has no further expense from this project — to compare them, an analyst must either capitalize the U.S. costs or expense the IFRS costs to achieve a common basis", "The European subsidiary is more conservative because capitalization defers costs to future periods, reducing current-period income", "Both subsidiaries should report identical financial results because GAAP and IFRS have converged on R&D treatment"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASC 730, all R&D costs are expensed as incurred — the U.S. subsidiary reports $3.6M in R&D expense in Year 2, reducing net income and creating no asset. Under IAS 38, development costs meeting six specific criteria (including technical feasibility, intention to complete, and ability to measure reliably) are capitalized and amortized. The European subsidiary capitalizes $3.6M as an intangible asset, reporting no expense in Year 2 but amortization in future periods. This creates divergent financial profiles from identical economic activity: the U.S. entity appears less profitable in Year 2 but has no future amortization burden, while the European entity defers the cost. R&D-to-revenue ratios, ROA, and asset turnover all diverge. Analysts must normalize by choosing a common treatment. (A) is wrong because the accounting difference materially affects key ratios and reported profitability. (C) reverses the relationship — expensing (U.S.) is more conservative than capitalizing (IFRS) in the current period. (D) is wrong because GAAP and IFRS have not converged on development cost capitalization.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 409;

-- ID 410: Intangible Assets — Technological feasibility milestone: effect on capitalization timing and income volatility
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Cascade Software is developing a new enterprise application for external sale. The project has three phases: (1) conceptual design ($200,000 spent), (2) detailed program design through working model ($450,000 spent), and (3) coding and testing after working model completion ($350,000 spent). The working model was completed at the end of Phase 2. Under ASC 985-20, how does the technological feasibility milestone affect the total amount capitalized versus expensed, and what analytical concern does the subjectivity of this milestone create for financial statement comparability across software companies?',
  choices = '["All $1,000,000 is expensed because software development costs are always treated as R&D under U.S. GAAP regardless of the project stage", "All $1,000,000 is capitalized because the company intends to sell the software externally, and the intent to sell triggers capitalization from the start of the project", "Phases 1 and 2 ($650,000) are expensed as R&D and Phase 3 ($350,000) is capitalized — the technological feasibility milestone (working model completion) is the dividing line, but this milestone is inherently subjective because companies define ''working model'' differently, meaning two companies with identical development spending could report materially different amounts of capitalized software depending on when each company determines feasibility was achieved", "Only Phase 1 ($200,000) is expensed and Phases 2-3 ($800,000) are capitalized because detailed program design is the earliest acceptable trigger for technological feasibility"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 985-20-25, costs incurred before technological feasibility is established are expensed as R&D. Technological feasibility is established upon completion of a detailed program design or a working model. Phases 1-2 ($650,000) predate the working model and are expensed. Phase 3 ($350,000) follows feasibility and is capitalized, then amortized using the greater of straight-line or revenue-proportion method. The analytical concern is real: the feasibility milestone is management''s judgment call. Company A might declare feasibility after a prototype, while Company B waits until beta testing — producing different capitalization amounts from identical projects. This subjectivity affects reported R&D expense, total assets, and net income, reducing cross-company comparability. Analysts must review capitalization policies in the notes to assess whether differences reflect real project maturity or aggressive accounting. (A) is wrong because ASC 985-20 explicitly permits capitalization after feasibility. (B) is wrong because intent to sell does not trigger capitalization — feasibility does. (D) is wrong because detailed program design establishes feasibility only if completed; merely starting it does not qualify.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 410;

