-- ID 1096: Cash and Receivables — Cash equivalent classification and its effect on liquidity reporting
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Corp.''s treasury department holds the following instruments as of December 31, 2025: a 60-day Treasury bill purchased at issuance ($2 million), a 120-day certificate of deposit purchased at issuance ($1.5 million), a money market fund ($800,000), and a 90-day commercial paper note purchased when it had 45 days remaining to maturity ($500,000). The controller must determine which instruments qualify as cash equivalents for the statement of cash flows. How does the classification of these instruments affect the reported cash and cash equivalents balance, and why does the original maturity criterion matter for liquidity analysis?',
  choices = '["All four instruments qualify as cash equivalents because they are all highly liquid, short-term investments that can be readily converted to cash — the total cash equivalents balance is $4.8 million", "Only the Treasury bill ($2M) and money market fund ($800K) qualify — the CD and commercial paper fail because their original maturities exceed 90 days — the $2.8 million cash equivalents balance means $2.0 million of liquid instruments are excluded from the cash position, which understates liquidity", "The Treasury bill ($2M), money market fund ($800K), and commercial paper ($500K) qualify because each had an original maturity of three months or less from the entity''s purchase date — the CD''s 120-day original maturity disqualifies it — this classification matters because cash equivalents signal near-cash liquidity on the balance sheet, and including longer-maturity instruments would overstate the company''s ability to meet immediate obligations", "Only the money market fund qualifies because it is the only instrument with daily liquidity — all fixed-maturity instruments are classified as short-term investments regardless of their original maturity"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (C): Under ASC 230-10-20, cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments with an original maturity of three months or less from the date of purchase. The Treasury bill (60-day original maturity) qualifies. The money market fund qualifies as it provides daily liquidity. The commercial paper (90 days original when purchased at 45 days remaining — but the question states it was purchased when it had 45 days remaining, and the original maturity of the paper itself was 90 days; however, the test is original maturity from the entity''s purchase date, which is 45 days) qualifies. The CD''s 120-day original maturity disqualifies it. The $1.5M CD is classified as a short-term investment, not a cash equivalent. The original maturity criterion prevents companies from inflating their cash position with instruments that may not be readily convertible without penalty. (A) is wrong because the 120-day CD fails the three-month test. (B) is wrong because the commercial paper qualifies based on 45 days remaining at purchase. (D) is wrong because fixed-maturity instruments with original maturities under 90 days do qualify.',
  correct_index = 2
WHERE id = 1096;

-- ID 1097: Cash and Receivables — Bank reconciliation adjustments and their differential effect on book vs bank balance
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Industries'' December 31 bank reconciliation reveals the following items: bank statement balance $124,500; outstanding checks $18,200; deposits in transit $9,800; NSF check from a customer $3,400; bank service charges $275; a note collected by the bank on Ashford''s behalf $12,000 (including $500 interest); and a bank error that credited Ashford''s account for another company''s $5,000 deposit. The controller must determine which items adjust the bank balance and which adjust the book balance. Why does this distinction matter for Ashford''s accounting records?',
  choices = '["All items adjust the bank balance because the bank reconciliation''s purpose is to correct the bank''s records to match the company''s books", "Outstanding checks ($18,200) and deposits in transit ($9,800) adjust the bank balance because they represent timing differences already recorded in Ashford''s books — the NSF check ($3,400), service charges ($275), and note collection ($12,000) adjust the book balance because Ashford has not yet recorded these transactions — the bank error ($5,000) adjusts the bank balance because it is the bank''s mistake — this distinction matters because only book-side adjustments require journal entries that change Ashford''s general ledger", "All items adjust the book balance because the bank statement is the authoritative record and the company must adjust its books to match", "Outstanding checks and the bank error adjust the book balance, while deposits in transit and the note collection adjust the bank balance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Bank reconciliation items fall into two categories. Bank-side adjustments correct the bank''s reported balance for items the company has already recorded: outstanding checks (issued and recorded by Ashford, not yet cleared by bank — subtract from bank) and deposits in transit (deposited and recorded by Ashford, not yet credited by bank — add to bank). The bank error is also a bank-side adjustment because it is the bank''s mistake to correct. Book-side adjustments reflect items the company has not yet recorded: the NSF check ($3,400 debit to A/R, credit to cash), service charges ($275 debit to expense, credit to cash), and note collection ($12,000 debit to cash, credit to notes receivable and interest revenue). The analytical significance is that only book-side items require journal entries in Ashford''s general ledger. The adjusted bank balance = $124,500 - $18,200 + $9,800 - $5,000 = $111,100. The adjusted book balance must equal $111,100 after recording the three book adjustments. (A) is wrong because the company''s recorded transactions (checks, deposits) do not need bank correction. (C) is wrong because the company has already correctly recorded timing-difference items.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1097;

-- ID 1098: Cash and Receivables — CECL vs incurred loss model effect on allowance timing and earnings volatility
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Waverly Financial holds a $50 million commercial loan portfolio. Under the old incurred loss model, the company recognized credit losses only when a loss event (such as borrower default or bankruptcy) had occurred, maintaining an allowance of $800,000. After adopting the CECL model under ASC 326, the company''s day-one allowance increases to $2.1 million based on lifetime expected losses incorporating historical data, current economic conditions, and reasonable forecasts of a mild recession. No actual defaults have occurred. How does the transition from incurred loss to CECL affect the timing of loss recognition and the company''s reported equity, and what analytical advantage does the earlier recognition provide?',
  choices = '["CECL has no effect on the allowance because no actual losses have occurred — the $800,000 allowance is appropriate under both models since the portfolio is fully performing", "CECL increases the day-one allowance by $1.3 million, with a corresponding reduction to retained earnings (through a cumulative-effect adjustment), recognizing expected losses before a triggering event occurs — this front-loads loss recognition, reducing the procyclical effect of the incurred loss model where large reserves were established only during downturns, and provides investors with an earlier signal of credit risk embedded in the portfolio", "CECL reduces the allowance because it uses forward-looking forecasts that predict lower losses than historical experience", "CECL increases the allowance by $1.3 million, but the increase is recognized as an expense on the income statement in the adoption year, reducing net income rather than retained earnings"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ASC 326-10-65-1 requires a cumulative-effect adjustment to retained earnings upon adoption — the $1.3 million increase ($2.1M - $0.8M) reduces retained earnings directly, not through the income statement. The CECL model recognizes lifetime expected credit losses at origination, incorporating forward-looking information, rather than waiting for an incurred loss trigger (default, bankruptcy, delinquency). This eliminates the procyclical pattern of the old model, where banks built reserves only after downturns had already begun, amplifying economic stress. For analysts, the CECL allowance provides a more transparent view of embedded credit risk in the portfolio. The $2.1 million allowance signals management''s expectation of future losses even though the portfolio currently performs, enabling earlier risk assessment. (A) is wrong because CECL explicitly does not require a loss event — expected losses are recognized from day one. (C) is wrong because CECL''s inclusion of current conditions and forecasts typically increases allowances relative to the incurred loss model. (D) is wrong because the transition adjustment goes through retained earnings, not the income statement.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1098;

