-- ID 1106: Employee Benefit Plans — Service cost classification under ASU 2017-07 and its effect on operating income analysis
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Greystone Manufacturing has net periodic pension cost of $850,000, composed of: service cost $320,000, interest cost $280,000, expected return on plan assets $(210,000), amortization of prior service cost $35,000, and amortization of net actuarial loss $425,000. Before ASU 2017-07, the entire $850,000 was reported as an operating expense. Under ASU 2017-07, only service cost is reported in operating income. How does the reclassification of the non-service components affect Greystone''s reported operating income, and what analytical challenge does this create for comparing Greystone to a competitor that sponsors no defined benefit plan?',
  choices = '["The reclassification has no effect on operating income because all pension cost components are employee compensation costs that belong in operating expenses regardless of ASU 2017-07", "The reclassification increases reported operating income by $530,000 (the non-service components moved below operating income) — this inflates Greystone''s operating margin relative to a competitor without a pension plan, creating a comparability illusion: Greystone''s $530,000 in non-service pension costs still exist but are now classified as non-operating, making its core operations appear more profitable than they are when pension obligations are economically part of its labor cost structure", "The reclassification decreases operating income because the expected return on plan assets ($210,000) is no longer offset against operating pension costs", "The reclassification has no analytical impact because investors and analysts always look at net income, not operating income, when evaluating company performance"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under ASU 2017-07 (ASC 715-30-35-83), only service cost ($320,000) is presented with other compensation costs in operating income. The remaining components — interest cost ($280,000), expected return on plan assets (-$210,000), prior service cost amortization ($35,000), and net loss amortization ($425,000) — totaling $530,000, are presented below operating income. This shifts $530,000 from operating to non-operating, increasing reported operating income by that amount. Analytically, this creates a comparability challenge: Greystone''s operating margin appears higher than a pension-free competitor''s, even though both companies bear their respective labor costs. Analysts must recognize that $530,000 of pension-related costs are embedded in Greystone''s cost structure but hidden below the operating line. (A) is wrong because ASU 2017-07 explicitly requires this bifurcated presentation. (C) is wrong because the expected return offset is moved along with all non-service components. (D) is wrong because operating income is a critical metric for ratio analysis, covenant compliance, and valuation multiples.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1106;

-- ID 1107: Employee Benefit Plans — Funded status reporting and its balance sheet implications for overfunded vs underfunded plans
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ashford Corp. sponsors two defined benefit pension plans. Plan A has a projected benefit obligation (PBO) of $12 million and plan assets with a fair value of $14 million. Plan B has a PBO of $8 million and plan assets of $5.5 million. The controller asks whether the $2 million overfunded status of Plan A can offset the $2.5 million underfunded status of Plan B for balance sheet presentation. Under ASC 715, how must the funded status of each plan be reported, and what does the separation reveal about the company''s overall pension risk?',
  choices = '["The two plans are netted, and Ashford reports a single net pension liability of $500,000 ($2.5M underfunded minus $2.0M overfunded) — this netting provides a clearer picture of total pension exposure", "Each plan''s funded status is reported separately — Plan A''s $2 million overfunding is reported as a noncurrent asset and Plan B''s $2.5 million underfunding is reported as a liability (allocated between current and noncurrent) — netting is prohibited because it would obscure the fact that Plan B carries significant underfunding risk, and the $2 million asset in Plan A is legally restricted to Plan A''s participants and cannot be used to settle Plan B''s obligations", "Both plans are reported as liabilities because pension obligations always represent net costs to the employer regardless of the funded status of individual plans", "The funded status is disclosed only in the notes — the balance sheet reports only the net periodic pension cost accrued during the current year"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): ASC 715-30-25-1 through 25-3 requires the funded status of each plan to be recognized separately on the balance sheet. Plan A''s $2 million surplus (FV of assets $14M minus PBO $12M) is reported as a noncurrent pension asset. Plan B''s $2.5 million deficit (FV $5.5M minus PBO $8M) is reported as a pension liability, with the portion expected to be paid within 12 months classified as current. Netting across plans is prohibited because the assets of one plan are legally segregated and cannot be used to fund another plan''s obligations. For risk analysis, the separate presentation reveals that despite appearing to have only a $500,000 net pension problem, Ashford actually has a severely underfunded Plan B requiring future contributions. (A) is wrong because ASC 715 prohibits offsetting overfunded and underfunded plans. (C) is wrong because overfunded plans are reported as assets. (D) is wrong because post-SFAS 158 (now ASC 715), the funded status must appear on the balance sheet, not just in notes.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1107;

-- ID 1108: Employee Benefit Plans — Defined contribution vs defined benefit risk allocation and financial statement effects
UPDATE questions SET
  stem = 'Ridgemont Manufacturing sponsors a 401(k) defined contribution plan with a 50% match on employee contributions up to 6% of salary. Its subsidiary, Ridgemont Precision, sponsors a traditional defined benefit pension plan with a PBO of $15 million and plan assets of $11 million. Both divisions have total payroll of $10 million. During a year when the stock market declines 20%, Ridgemont Manufacturing''s pension expense is unchanged while Ridgemont Precision records a $2.4 million actuarial loss in other comprehensive income. How does the risk allocation differ between the two plan types, and what are the divergent financial statement effects of the market decline?',
  choices = '["Both plan types expose the employer to the same investment risk — the only difference is the accounting presentation, not the economic substance", "The defined contribution plan shifts investment risk entirely to employees — the employer''s obligation ends at the matching contribution, so the market decline has no effect on Ridgemont Manufacturing''s financial statements — the defined benefit plan retains investment risk with the employer because Ridgemont Precision must fund the promised benefits regardless of asset returns, and the $2.4 million actuarial loss (recognized in OCI under ASC 715) increases the underfunded liability from $4 million to $6.4 million, potentially triggering larger future contributions and amortization charges to pension expense", "The defined contribution plan exposes the employer to greater risk because matching contributions increase as employees contribute more to recover market losses", "The defined benefit plan is less risky for the employer because the pension trust absorbs all investment losses, with no effect on the company''s financial statements"]'::jsonb,
  explanation = 'Correct (B): Under a defined contribution plan (ASC 715-70), the employer''s obligation is limited to the specified contribution — once the match is deposited, investment risk transfers to the employee. A 20% market decline affects employee account balances, not the employer''s financial statements. Under a defined benefit plan (ASC 715-30), the employer promises a future benefit amount regardless of investment performance. When plan assets decline by 20% (roughly $2.2M on $11M), the funded status deteriorates because the PBO remains unchanged. The actuarial loss ($2.4M, reflecting the asset loss plus possible discount rate changes) is recognized in OCI per ASC 715-30-35-26 and increases the net pension liability on the balance sheet. This loss enters the corridor calculation and may increase future pension expense through amortization. The employer may also need to increase cash contributions to meet minimum funding requirements. (A) is wrong because the risk allocation differs fundamentally. (C) is wrong because matching formulas are based on salary percentages, not market performance. (D) is wrong because investment losses directly affect the employer''s funded status and financial statements.',
  correct_index = 1
WHERE id = 1108;

COMMIT;
